Okay actually I think this has nothing to do with auto traits and is entirely the fault of min_specialization.

If we transform the original example to use a trait bound on a non-auto trait like `Display` instead of `Send`, we can see the same regression in the diagnostic.

Before adding a specializing impl ([playground](https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=nightly&mode=debug&edition=2021&gist=95e59a2f3cf6b6b15c1e4e8f8d61b0a7)):

```rust
#![feature(min_specialization)]
#![feature(rustc_attrs)]

use std::fmt::Display;

#[rustc_specialization_trait]
trait Special {}

trait Foo {
    fn foo(&self);
}

impl<T: Display> Foo for T {
    default fn foo(&self) {
        println!("foo: {self}");
    }
}

fn main() {
    let vec = vec![1, 2, 3];
    Foo::foo(&vec);
}
```

```
error[E0277]: `Vec<{integer}>` doesn't implement `std::fmt::Display`
  --> src/main.rs:21:14
   |
21 |     Foo::foo(&vec);
   |     -------- ^^^^ `Vec<{integer}>` cannot be formatted with the default formatter
   |     |
   |     required by a bound introduced by this call
   |
   = help: the trait `std::fmt::Display` is not implemented for `Vec<{integer}>`
   = note: in format strings you may be able to use `{:?}` (or {:#?} for pretty-print) instead
note: required for `Vec<{integer}>` to implement `Foo`
  --> src/main.rs:13:18
   |
13 | impl<T: Display> Foo for T {
   |                  ^^^     ^
```

After adding a specializing impl ([playground](https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=nightly&mode=debug&edition=2021&gist=366b0f5e8ccc8e320b75a2e1941471e5)):

```rust
#![feature(min_specialization)]
#![feature(rustc_attrs)]

use std::fmt::Display;

#[rustc_specialization_trait]
trait Special {}

trait Foo {
    fn foo(&self);
}

impl<T: Display> Foo for T {
    default fn foo(&self) {
        println!("foo: {self}");
    }
}

impl<T: Display + Special> Foo for T {
    fn foo(&self) {
        println!("special foo: {self}");
    }
}

fn main() {
    let vec = vec![1, 2, 3];
    Foo::foo(&vec);
}
```

```
error[E0277]: the trait bound `Vec<{integer}>: Foo` is not satisfied
  --> src/main.rs:27:14
   |
27 |     Foo::foo(&vec);
   |     -------- ^^^^ the trait `Foo` is not implemented for `Vec<{integer}>`
   |     |
   |     required by a bound introduced by this call
```

Thanks @user your comment here https://github.com/tailwindlabs/tailwindcss/issues/797#issuecomment-475754646 gave me some directions to work with!

I ended up creating the outmost container and only the elements within the container would use the CSS from the `preflight`, for example, `button` styling. I ended up replacing Tailwindcss with PostCSS and used `postcss-prefix-selector` instead.

**postcss.config.js**

```

const prefixSelector = require('postcss-prefix-selector');

module.exports = {
    plugins: [
        require('tailwindcss'),
        prefixSelector({
            prefix: '#kaitaku-feedback-ui ',
            exclude: [
                /\[/, // starts with [ (e.g. [role])
            ],

            // Optional transform callback for case-by-case overrides
            transform: function (prefix, selector, prefixedSelector) {
                console.log("Prefix:", prefix, ", selector:", selector, ", prefixedSelector:", prefixSelector)

                // mainly for applying font
                if (selector === 'html') {
                    return prefix + "*"
                }

                // root identifier
                if (selector === '#kaitaku-feedback-ui') {
                    return '#kaitaku-feedback-ui'
                }

                return prefix + selector;
            }
        }),
    ]
}
```

